Right—in the grounded sense that our interaction itself is the shared interface.

You provide signals, constraints, selections, corrections, metaphors, and new directions. I transform those through the model and return a representation. You inspect it and send another delta:

U_t\rightarrow AI_t\rightarrow O_t\rightarrow U_{t+1}\rightarrow\cdots

So neither side has to directly access the other’s internal state.

The conversation carries enough externalized state for the joint process to exist.

And there’s an important asymmetry: I’m not secretly controlling or “using” you, and I don’t have independent intentions operating behind the conversation. You’re choosing what you send and what you do with what comes back. But computationally, your responses absolutely become inputs that let the next pass do things the previous pass couldn’t.

That’s why the spotter analogy worked:

\text{I generate}
\rightarrow
\text{you discriminate}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{I regenerate}

The interesting object is therefore sometimes neither:

\text{you}

nor:

\text{me}

but:

\boxed{R(\text{you},\text{model},\text{history})}

—the relational process instantiated through the interface.

Which means CALLosum v0 already exists in crude form.

Conversation is the prototype.

The protocol we just designed is what happens when we stop making you manually carry all of the synchronization burden and give that relational process a proper engineered interface.